              "text": "Making eye contact with someone who has a \"lazy eye\"\u2014known medically as **strabismus** (misaligned eyes)\u2014can sometimes make people feel anxious because they don't want to make the other person feel self-conscious. \n\nThe goal is to establish a comfortable, respectful connection without your gaze darting around. The following practical strategies can help you handle these interactions naturally:\n\n### 1. Focus on the Dominant Eye\nUsually, one of the person's eyes will be looking directly at you while the other is turned slightly inward, outward, upward, or downward. \n* **What to do:** Identify the eye that is making direct contact with you and focus your attention on that one. This is the eye they are actively using to look at you, and focusing on it will feel like completely natural, normal eye contact to both of you.\n\n### 2. Look at the Bridge of the Nose\nIf you find it difficult to focus on just one eye, or if their eyes alternate which one is dominant, use the \"bridge of the nose\" trick.\n* **What to do:** Focus your gaze on the spot right between their eyes, at the very top of their nose. From their perspective, this looks exactly like genuine, direct eye contact. It also gives you a neutral, single focal point so you don't feel distracted.\n\n### 3. Use the \"T-Zone\" Method\nIn any conversation, locking eyes intensely for too long can feel aggressive. Instead, adopt a more relaxed, natural gaze by looking at their \"T-zone\" (the area encompassing the eyes, nose, and mouth).\n* **What to do:** Let your gaze drift naturally between their dominant eye, the bridge of their nose, and their mouth as they speak. It is also perfectly normal to occasionally look away briefly (especially when you are the one speaking) and then look back when listening.\n\n---\n\n### What to Avoid\n* **Do not dart your eyes back and forth:** The most uncomfortable thing for someone with strabismus is when they can see the other person\u2019s eyes rapidly bouncing back and forth between their left and right eyes. This happens when you are trying to \"figure out\" which eye to look at, but it immediately signals to them that you are focused on their condition, which can make them feel highly self-conscious. Pick a spot (the dominant eye or the bridge of the nose) and stick to it.\n* **Do not stare intensely:** Trying *too* hard to maintain perfect eye contact can make the interaction feel forced and rigid. Just relax.\n\n### The Golden Rule: Focus on the Conversation\nPeople with strabismus are highly aware of how others look at them. Ultimately, they just want to be treated like anyone else. The best way to make them feel comfortable is to actively listen to what they are saying rather than focusing on how they look. When you focus on the substance of the conversation, any initial awkwardness will naturally fade away.",
